ur of a kind) or two 2s or two Aces. But you **do** have the rocket.\n\nTherefore, the **only legal non-pass move** from your hand that beats the current play is the rocket.\n\nPassing would mean letting the teammate\'s play stand unchallenged, which could allow the landlord to regain lead — but only if both peasants pass. However, since you are allowed to play, and you have the rocket, passing here risks giving up control unnecessarily.\n\nBut consider: Why did your teammate play KK? Possibly to force out high cards or test reactions. The landlord may be holding onto bombs or waiting for an opportunity. By playing the rocket now, you guarantee control — you win the trick and get to lead next.\n\nGiven that you have no other way to beat the pair of Kings, and you want to support your teammate’s aggression, using the rocket here ensures dominance.\n\nHowever — rockets are extremely valuable and often saved for critical moments (e.g., protecting a teammate who is about to go out). Using it here might be premature unless necessary.\n\nBut critically: **Are there any other valid moves available besides rocket and pass?**\n\nLooking at the Valid Moves list provided:\n- Only two options:  \n  - rocket: BlackJoker RedJoker  \n  - pass: []\n\nThis means the game engine has evaluated your hand and determined that the **only** combinations you can legally play to beat the pair of Kings are the rocket or nothing (pass).\n\nSo despite having many cards, none form a higher pair (AA, 22), nor do you have a bomb (four-of-a-kind), so those aren\'t options.\n\nThus, your decision is binary: rocket or pass.\n\nNow, consider strategy:\n\n- If you pass, the landlord gets to play next (after both peasants pass), and they will likely gain control.\n- Playing the rocket gives you control — you win the trick and then get to lead any card or combo next.\n- With a wide range of mid-low cards and potential sequences (e.g., 3-8 straight nearly complete), after winning control you can lead a small sequence to help cycle through your hand and potentially set up your teammate.\n\nAlso, note: you hold both jokers — meaning no one else does. No one can beat your rocket once played. It\'s safe to use when needed.\n\nGiven that your teammate initiated a high play, supporting them by securing control now may disrupt the landlord’s rhythm.\n\nMoreover, delaying the rocket too long risks being forced out later under worse conditions (e.g., landlord leading bombs).\n\nThus, **now is a reasonable time** to play it — especially since it\'s the only way to maintain momentum.\n\nReason: The only way to beat the current pair of Kings is with the rocket, as I lack a higher natural pair (AA, 22) or a bomb. Passing would surrender control to the landlord. Playing the rocket secures the lead, allowing me to dictate the pace and support my teammate. Given that I hold both jokers, it\'s safe to use now to maintain strategic initiative.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["BlackJoker", "RedJoker"]}'}
